### 8.2 Material clipboard

```cpp
enum class MaterialClipboardSyntax : std::uint8_t {
  absent,
  malformed,
  parsed
};

struct ParsedMaterialClipboard {
  MaterialClipboardSyntax syntax;
  std::optional<std::uint64_t> documentIndex;
};

struct MaterialPasteAvailability {
  ParsedMaterialClipboard parsed;
  bool menuEnabled;
  bool indexInActiveDocument;
  std::optional<MaterialId> activeDocumentMaterial;
};

class IOsTextClipboard {
public:
  virtual ~IOsTextClipboard() = default;
  virtual std::optional<std::string> readText() const = 0;
  virtual bool writeText(std::string_view) = 0;
};
```

Codec and UI rules:

- Copy resolves `MaterialId` to its current document index at copy time and writes exact ASCII `gltf_material:<unsigned decimal>`, with no whitespace or stable identity.
- Parse requires the exact prefix and a nonempty, complete ASCII decimal suffix. Reject signs, leading/trailing spaces, junk, decimal points, non-ASCII digits, and checked-integer overflow. Leading zeroes are accepted by the D16 complete-decimal grammar but are never emitted by Copy.
- Wrong prefix, empty clipboard, empty suffix, malformed suffix, or overflow produces disabled Paste and no state change.
- A syntactically valid index enables Paste even when out of range, matching the reference UI. It shows invalid feedback and no-ops on click.
- On every click, resolve the index again against the currently active document. A document switch may make the same number valid, invalid, or refer to a different material; no prior `MaterialId` may be retained.
- Valid Paste/Assign uses the active-document material and remains outside editor history per the locked reference behavior. Same-material assignment is an idempotent no-op.
- Clipboard adapter code may call upstream Dear ImGui clipboard functions. The codec, selection service, and tests do not depend on Dear ImGui.

## 9. Highlight Contract

`EditorHighlightState` is derived renderer state, never selection authority:

```cpp
struct EditorHighlightState {
  EditorSceneFence fence;
  SelectionRevision selectionRevision;
  std::vector<RenderOccurrenceId> occurrences;
};
```

Rules:

- Node: recursively enumerate the selected Node and descendants in document child order, then include every live visible render occurrence.
- Primitive: include exactly the selected `PrimitiveOccurrenceKey`. If one logical occurrence expands to multiple internal draw fragments, all fragments share its pick/highlight token but remain one selected occurrence.
- Mesh, Material, Camera, Light, and empty selection: no silhouette in P6 unless a later locked requirement adds one.
- Hidden selected object: selection/Inspector may remain, but occurrence list is empty.
- Deleted/stale selection: reconciliation clears it; an old highlight fence is rejected.
- A highlight update is accepted only when both the scene-publication fence and selection revision match current state. Device/scene/projection swaps clear old highlight before the new publication is displayed; view motion and viewport resize do not invalidate the logical occurrence set.
- ObjectID plus post-process outline, a dedicated selection mask, or an equivalent RHI implementation is allowed. The behavioral contract is the occurrence set and resulting visible outline, not the reference Vulkan implementation.

## 10. Input Arbitration and DPI Coordinates

One MGIF-owned router receives an input snapshot after Dear ImGui has begun its frame and established capture state. Each event has at most one owner, in this fixed order:

1. modal/dialog;
2. active gizmo;
3. Dear ImGui captured widget/text input;
4. eligible viewport picking;
5. viewport camera navigation.

Router rules:

- A gizmo gesture that begins inside the viewport retains pointer capture outside it and receives the actual or explicitly synthesized release.
- Escape always clears selection. Escape-only detaches while retaining preview TRS and adds no transform-end history. Escape+release in one frame must deliver release/end-drag at most once and finish with empty selection, but its transform-history postcondition is blocked by the R16/O06 conflict documented in Section 2; the router must expose deterministic event order without hard-coding either disputed history result until the locked artifacts are reconciled.
- Right/middle buttons, wheel, and W/A/S/D/Q/E camera navigation run only when the viewport is eligible, no higher owner consumed the event, and text/keyboard capture is false.
- Picking never runs while the gizmo is active/dragging or while a modal/widget owns the pointer.
- Move current pre-ImGui GLFW camera polling behind the ImGui input snapshot. Do not infer capture from stale prior-frame `WantCapture*` values.

Coordinate conversion uses two explicit spaces:

```cpp
struct ViewportPresentation {
  double logicalMinX, logicalMinY;
  double logicalWidth, logicalHeight; // Dear ImGui screen coordinates
  std::uint32_t pixelWidth, pixelHeight; // actual rendered attachment
  std::uint64_t viewportGeneration;
  ViewportSurfaceTransform surfaceTransform;
};
```

For a pointer inside the half-open logical rectangle:

```text
u = (mouseX - logicalMinX) / logicalWidth
v = (mouseY - logicalMinY) / logicalHeight
pixelX = min(floor(u * pixelWidth),  pixelWidth  - 1)
pixelY = min(floor(v * pixelHeight), pixelHeight - 1)
```

Reject zero/non-finite logical dimensions, zero pixel extent, and points outside the half-open rectangle. Do not multiply by OS DPI after normalizing; the attachment extent already incorporates pixel density. Y inversion, rotation, or backend origin conventions belong to `ViewportSurfaceTransform` inside the RHI pick path, not app conditionals. A resize or attachment replacement increments `viewportGeneration`; a result from the prior extent is stale.

